        "level": 3
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Taxonomy"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-two-broad-archetypes-s-30": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-two-broad-archetypes-s-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Two broad archetypes: "
        },
        {
          "insert": "singleOutput",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "workflow",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". "
        },
        {
          "insert": "Steps",
          "attributes": {
            "bold": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " describe internal reasoning inside one completion; "
        },
        {
          "insert": "phases",
          "attributes": {
            "bold": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " describe external execution across tools, state, turns, and subagents, which keeps the taxonomy close to the kernel's runtime reality, where every run executes an agent definition but some definitions are simple enough to behave like a single task."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-three-terms-with-distinc-31": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-three-terms-with-distinc-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Two terms with distinct meanings: an "
        },
        {
          "insert": "archetype",
          "attributes": {
            "italic": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is a broad execution/structure family, stored in the document's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "archetype",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " field; an "
        },
        {
          "insert": "example",
          "attributes": {
            "italic": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is a concrete prompt in an actual app. Concrete coordinator or scout prompts are examples, never archetypes. Prompt-kit ships no templates. Extraction, evaluation, classification, critique/revise, and summarization are prompt patterns, not archetypes."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-variables-37": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-variables-37",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Variables"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-runtime-variables-are-de-38": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-runtime-variables-are-de-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Runtime variables are declared in the manifest, because they are part of the agent's runtime contract. A variable is optional unless its declaration sets "
        },
        {
          "insert": "required: true",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". The "
        },
        {
          "insert": "variables",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " field of the manifest shape gives the rule. The prompt references variables through typed variable nodes. Registry boot fails when a prompt references an undeclared variable, or when a declaration sets both "
        },
        {
          "insert": "required: true",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "optional: true",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". A spawn fails with "
        },
        {
          "insert": "MISSING_REQUIRED_VARIABLES",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " when a required variable has neither a caller-provided value nor a default. An optional variable with no value renders as an empty string. Caller variables with undeclared names pass through to the resolved set."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-context-44": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-context-44",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Context"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-context-ts-owns-dynami-45": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-context-ts-owns-dynami-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "context.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " owns dynamic context shape, loader declarations, and context rendering. The prompt owns instructions for how to "
        },
        {
          "insert": "use",
          "attributes": {
            "italic": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " the injected context. The prompt may reference a context block by id/tag with usage rules, but it never redeclares the schema "
        },
        {
          "insert": "context.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " already declares. Double-counting makes traces noisy and lets copies drift."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-the-contract-around-load-46": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-the-contract-around-load-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The contract around loaders:"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-context-ts-supports-a-47": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-context-ts-supports-a-47",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "context.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " supports a runtime-bound factory pattern, like "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tools.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", so app-owned services, paths, and loader catalogs inject cleanly while the bound result still matches the kernel's resolver contract."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-context-is-structured-fi-48": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-context-is-structured-fi-48",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Context is structured-first with a raw-string escape hatch. Typed helper builders sit on top of generic loader declarations, and the kernel operates on the generic form."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-loader-helpers-may-carry-49": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-loader-helpers-may-carry-49",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Loader declarations are small. The kernel ships five kinds ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "file",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "directory",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "skill",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "command",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "text",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "), and an app can register custom kinds. Declarations carry no rendering policy. The sidecar's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "assemble",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " function decides how loaded content renders."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-context-sections-are-opt-50": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-context-sections-are-opt-50",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Context sections are optional by default. Each loader reports a status of "
        },
        {
          "insert": "ok",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "empty",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", or "
        },
        {
          "insert": "error",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "packages/kernel/src/context/types.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "). A sidecar declares "
        },
        {
          "insert": "defineContext({ loaders, assemble })",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "assemble",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " receives every status and decides what to render. Unlike variables, external data sources are often legitimately empty."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-problems-surface-through-51": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-problems-surface-through-51",
      "type": "list-item",
      "props": {
        "ordered": false
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Problems surface through both boot validation and runtime trace events. Boot validation eagerly checks what is knowable before a run: catalog shape, duplicate section ids, malformed configs, package-relative resources. Session-relative files, app state, database-backed context, and working memory are checked at runtime. Each loader result is traced with its status, and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "assemble",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " decides whether an empty or failed input matters."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-under-the-state-model-t-52": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-under-the-state-model-t-52",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Under the state model, the boundary between reference material and working state is drawn, not blurry: "
        },
        {
          "insert": "context.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " declares section ②, reference material visible on every request, and no longer holds working state, working memory, or conversation slices, which are section ③, owned by the state sidecar (D81–D84, "
        },
        {
          "insert": "State Model",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "doc",
              "path": "10-system-design/60-prompt-system-model/30-state-model"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ")."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-tools-53": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-tools-53",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Tools"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-the-manifest-declares-sh-54": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-the-manifest-declares-sh-54",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The manifest declares access to tools that exist outside the agent: core runtime tools, shared app tools, and provider or MCP tools. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "coreTools",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " lists allowed tool names. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "toolProfiles",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " names profiles that the registry expands into tool names from its configured profile map. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "disallowedTools",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " removes names. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "extensions",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is "
        },
        {
          "insert": "true",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "false",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", or a list of extension names to keep. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tools.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " defines private tools that belong to the agent. A tool defined there is part of the agent's capability surface and is never repeated in an allowlist. Shared access is permission and configuration. Private tools are implementation plus declaration."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-private-tool-definitions-55": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-private-tool-definitions-55",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Private tool definitions stay directly compatible with Pi's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "registerTool(...)",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " shape, so registration is boring and reliable. A "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tools.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " sidecar exports "
        },
        {
          "insert": "defineTools((pi, runtime) => { ... })",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", a runtime-bound factory that calls "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pi.registerTool",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " for each tool ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "packages/kernel/src/agent-definition/index.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "). App services inject through "
        },
        {
          "insert": "runtime",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " while the result stays ordinary Pi tool definitions. Tool metadata does not need to be statically inspectable without a runtime; validation binds a real or stub runtime when the registry or viewer needs metadata."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-spawner-tools-56": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-spawner-tools-56",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 3
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Spawner tools"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-spawning-is-granted-per-57": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-spawning-is-granted-per-57",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Spawning is granted per tool, not per agent. There is no manifest-level "
        },
        {
          "insert": "canSpawnSubagent",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " boolean. A tool that dispatches subagents is declared with "
        },
        {
          "insert": "defineSpawnerTool({ ..., spawns: [agent names] })",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " in "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tools.ts",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". The kernel injects a scoped "
        },
        {
          "insert": "dispatch",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " handle at session build time that enforces the declared allowlist and auto-forwards "
        },
        {
          "insert": "parentToolUseId",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "trigger: \"parent-tool\"",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and run-context identity."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-agent-platforms-default-58": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-agent-platforms-default-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Agent platforms default to \"everything can spawn general subagents\". This kernel does not. A permission boolean says an agent may spawn, but not what, through which tool, or with what identity plumbing. Spawner declarations are harvested at registry boot. Every non-"
        },
        {
          "insert": "\"*\"",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " target must exist in the catalog or boot fails. The harvested map rides on the runtime config. A deliberately general spawner remains possible via "
        },
        {
          "insert": "spawns: [\"*\"]",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", but it is a loud opt-in visible in the declaration, the harvest, and the trace. Spawner calls are distinguishable in traces: "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tool_call_start",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " / "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tool_call_end",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " eventData gains optional "
        },
        {
          "insert": "toolKind: \"spawner\"",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " + "
        },
        {
          "insert": "spawns",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", additive optional fields on the existing payloads, no new event types, no envelope change, so viewers can render agent dispatch differently from ordinary tools."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-variants-and-run-overrid-59": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-variants-and-run-overrid-59",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Variants and Run Overrides"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-durable-agent-definition-60": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-durable-agent-definition-60",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Durable agent definitions own relatively stable configuration: name, description, model and settings, prompt source, variable schema, context assembler, private tool registrations, spawn declarations, turn limits, background behavior. Run invocations own dynamic values: caller-provided variables, the current message or assignment, session identity and paths, runtime inputs, parent/subagent linkage."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-run-invocations-casually-61": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-run-invocations-casually-61",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Run invocations casually override only the dynamic inputs. Prompt structure, tool access, variable schema, context shape, spawn declarations, model, thinking level, max turns, and background behavior are configuration and cost/control semantics. Differing values are an explicit variant or an administrative override, not ordinary run input."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-10-dab-variants-represent-diffe-62": {
      "id": "b-10-dab-variants-represent-diffe-62",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Variants represent different configurations of the same prompt, context, and tool identity: split testing, cost and performance tuning, phase-specific operational settings. The manifest's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "variants",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " field lists what a variant may set. If behavior diverges materially, that is a distinct agent, not a variant. Prompt A/B testing is out of scope for the core variant model; if it matters later it is separate experiment tooling or explicit temporary agents."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    }
  }
}
